# Changelog

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:10:27Z

## Purpose

Maintain a concise project change history in the root `CHANGELOG.md`.
Help readers understand what meaningfully changed and its practical effect.

The changelog is not a daily activity log, task tracker, or copy of Git history.
Coursework follows its applicable course instructions.

## When to add an entry

Record meaningful:
- New capabilities and behavior changes.
- Fixes affecting correctness or use.
- Compatibility changes, deprecations, and removals.
- Changes affecting setup or project workflow.

Skip typo corrections, formatting, routine internal edits, and dependency updates
with no meaningful impact.

Add or consolidate entries at coherent task checkpoints rather than after
every edit. Group related changes into a useful outcome.

## Organization

Use a short purpose statement followed by dated sections, newest first.
Use `YYYY-MM-DD` headings under the conventions in
[dates_and_times.mdc](dates_and_times.mdc).

Add categories such as Added, Changed, Fixed, or Deprecated only when they
improve readability. Omit empty categories.

Follow [documentation_core.mdc](documentation_core.mdc) for document timestamps
and freshness. Keep detailed changelog policy here rather than copying it into
`CHANGELOG.md`.

Do not introduce release versions or a release process before they are needed.
Do not reconstruct every historical commit when establishing the changelog.

## Entry content

State the outcome and practical effect in concise, factual language.
Identify breaking changes or required user action clearly.

Describe delivered behavior accurately. Do not present plans, incomplete work,
or unverified capabilities as completed changes.

Link to relevant ADRs for rationale, commits for implementation, and existing
verification records when useful. Include commit references only when available;
do not delay an otherwise useful entry solely to obtain a hash.

Do not copy decision narratives, task records, or test logs into entries.
A verification subsection is not required for every change.

